<item>
  <title>Do more homeruns equal more fans?</title>
  <dc:creator>Blake Lorenzen</dc:creator>
  <link>https://your-website-url.example.com/posts/baseball-homerun-wordseries-viewership/</link>
  <description><![CDATA[ 





<p>Major League Baseball has seen more home runs over the past several decades, but those big hits haven’t brought more viewers to the World Series. Television audiences for MLB have dropped significantly since the 1970s, despite an increase in home runs. Data from Baseball Almanac and Baseball Reference show how baseball’s biggest event has struggled to bring in viewers.</p>
<p>Millions of people tuned in to watch the World Series in the 1970s. From 1973 to 1979, baseball’s biggest stage averaged approximately 36.3 million viewers, with the most being in 1978 with 44.3 million viewers. By 2023, that number had fallen to 9.1 million, showing a dramatic decline. The overall trend shows a steady decline in viewership over the past five decades.</p>
<p>While World Series viewership has plummeted, MLB teams have increased their home run rates. The data shows a negative relationship between home runs per team and World Series television viewership. Homerun’s still remain one of baseball’s most exciting plays, but hitting more of them has not reversed the steady decline of viewership.</p>
